te at one point in time  the ancestors of the associates did have  mouths but as they found their way into  worms and specifically their guts those  oral grooves became less necessary  instead the ciliates could rely on a  form of feeding called osmotrophy where  they simply absorb nutrients from their  surroundings through osmosis instead of  taking in larger bits of food through  their mouths and breaking it down  themselves as tometid ciliates could  just take advantage of the worm's  digestive system to do all of that  breaking down for them as the worms  digestive enzymes break down complex  molecules into simpler forms that can  travel through their own intestinal  walls some of those nutrients would just  go to feed the aztoma did ciliate  instead  these ciliates will actually sometimes  be picky about making sure they're in a  particular spot within their host's  intestinal tract and once they've found  the right spot the ciliates will hold  themselves in place with organelles that  range in shape some use hooks others  spines maybe even suckers  the flat shape of the ciliates helps  them stay pressed to the epithelium of  the intestines so while these ciliates  may not need a mouth anymore they have  found other traits necessary to their  survival  as tomated ciliates are found in hosts  from all sorts of environments some live  in soil some live in ponds some even  live in ocean waters and scientists are  using the general tools available now to  try and piece together how host and  endosymbiont have shaped each other and  we can see some of that intertwined  story in the ciliates mouthlessness but  the specifics of that evolutionary  change are laid out in their dna  as are the more hidden parts of that  shared history  now we cannot know whether the worms  have any feelings about their guts being  home to another organism they likely do  not have much choice in the matter but  they also likely are not prone to  emotions like resentment and the way  that 